Most scans use the same surrounding lifecycle structure.
Add:
@scan_hook
def open_scan(self):
self.actions.open_scan()
@scan_hook
def stage(self):
self.actions.stage_all_devices()
@scan_hook
def pre_scan(self):
self.actions.pre_scan()
@scan_hook
def unstage(self):
self.actions.unstage_all_devices()
@scan_hook
def close_scan(self):
if self._baseline_readout_status is not None:
self._baseline_readout_status.wait()
self.actions.close_scan()
self.actions.check_for_unchecked_statuses()
These hooks are part of the shared scan shape. They are one reason scans in BEC tend to feel familiar even when the actual motion pattern changes.

Now add the execution logic.
@scan_hook
def scan_core(self):
self.components.step_scan(self.motors, self.positions, at_each_point=self.at_each_point)
@scan_hook
def at_each_point(self, motors, positions, last_positions):
self.components.step_scan_at_each_point(
motors, positions, last_positions=last_positions
)
This reuses shared scan components instead of reimplementing the point-by-point logic yourself.
What this step means:
scan_core runs the scan
at_each_point defines what happens per point
the shared components handle the standard step-scan behavior

Add:
@scan_hook
def post_scan(self):
status = self.actions.complete_all_devices(wait=False)
status.wait()
This scan is absolute, so it does not move back to the starting position.

At this point, your scan should look like this:
from typing import Annotated
import numpy as np
from bec_lib.device import DeviceBase
from bec_server.scan_server.scans import ScanBase, ScanType, Units, scan_hook
class SimpleLineScan(ScanBase):
scan_type = ScanType.SOFTWARE_TRIGGERED
scan_name = "simple_line_scan"
gui_config = {
"Device": ["motor"],
"Movement Parameters": ["start", "stop", "steps"],
"Acquisition Parameters": ["exp_time"],
}
def __init__(
self,
motor: DeviceBase,
start: Annotated[float, "motor"],
stop: Annotated[float, "motor"],
steps: int,
exp_time: Annotated[float, Units.s] = 0,
**kwargs,
):
super().__init__(**kwargs)
self.motors = [motor]
self.start = start
self.stop = stop
self.steps = steps
self.exp_time = exp_time
self._baseline_readout_status = None
self.update_scan_info(exp_time=exp_time)
self.actions.set_device_readout_priority(self.motors, priority="monitored")
@scan_hook
def prepare_scan(self):
self.positions = np.linspace(self.start, self.stop, self.steps).reshape(-1, 1)
self.components.check_limits(self.motors, self.positions)
self.actions.add_scan_report_instruction_scan_progress(
points=len(self.positions), show_table=False
)
self.update_scan_info(
start=self.start,
stop=self.stop,
steps=self.steps,
positions=self.positions,
num_points=len(self.positions),
)
self._baseline_readout_status = self.actions.read_baseline_devices(wait=False)
@scan_hook
def open_scan(self):
self.actions.open_scan()
@scan_hook
def stage(self):
self.actions.stage_all_devices()
@scan_hook
def pre_scan(self):
self.actions.pre_scan()
@scan_hook
def scan_core(self):
self.components.step_scan(self.motors, self.positions, at_each_point=self.at_each_point)
@scan_hook
def at_each_point(self, motors, positions, last_positions):
self.components.step_scan_at_each_point(
motors, positions, last_positions=last_positions
)
@scan_hook
def post_scan(self):
status = self.actions.complete_all_devices(wait=False)
status.wait()
@scan_hook
def unstage(self):
self.actions.unstage_all_devices()
@scan_hook
def close_scan(self):
if self._baseline_readout_status is not None:
self._baseline_readout_status.wait()
self.actions.close_scan()
self.actions.check_for_unchecked_statuses()

BEC launcher
The launcher offers three application modes. To keep the first steps simple, choose Terminal. This opens the BECIPythonClient in a terminal, which will be used to learn the basic BEC tools in the following tutorial pages.
///
/// tab :material-laptop: Local environment outside PSI

Now you can start the BEC client without the GUI using the bec command:
bec --nogui
///

Overview
Choosing Kind for a signal in your custom ophyd device determines how BEC will handle the signal and its associated data.
[Learn more about ophyd](../../learn/devices/introduction-to-ophyd.md){ data-preview }

You have defined a device in your BEC device config file, either through the YAML config or through the GUI.
The read() and read_configuration() methods of your device return the expected signals and values.
[Consult the ophyd kinds documentation](../../learn/devices/ophyd-kinds.md){ data-preview }

BEC is running with the simulated bpm4i device.
The device exposes the simulation interface as dev.bpm4i.sim.
[Learn how simulated devices work](../../learn/devices/simulated-devices.md){ data-preview }

A beamline plugin repository is available and installed.
Write access (beamline manager) to the beamline plugin package.
[Learn about file writing in BEC](../../learn/file-writer/introduction.md){ data-preview }

Create a new module in your plugin under .file_writer. .
from bec_server.file_writer.default_writer import DefaultFormat
class BeamlineNeXusFormat(DefaultFormat):
"""Beamline-specific NeXuS structure for BEC output files."""
def format(self) -> None:
pass

Choose the environment style that matches your usual workflow.
/// tab pyenv
If you use pyenv together with pyenv-virtualenv, create a dedicated environment:
pyenv install 3.11
pyenv virtualenv 3.11 bec
pyenv local bec
///
/// tab Python virtual environment
If you already have Python 3.11 or newer installed:
python -m venv ./bec_venv
source ./bec_venv/bin/activate
///
/// tab Conda
If you use Conda:
conda create -n bec python=3.11
conda activate bec
///

Opening the correct folder is important for VS Code to work well. We highly recommend opening the deployment directory:
/// tab Template
cd /sls/ /config/bec/
code .
///
/// tab Example
cd /sls/x01da/config/bec/production
code .
///
This ensures that VS Code finds the already prepared settings.json file with the correct Python environment.
RecommendedExtensions

dap_name can also be a list of model names when you want a composite model:
wf.add_dap_curve(
device_label="bpm4i-bpm4i",
dap_name=["GaussianModel", "LinearModel"],
)
You can pass parameter overrides with dap_parameters. For composite models, pass either a list aligned with the model
list or a dictionary keyed by model name:
wf.add_dap_curve(
device_label="bpm4i-bpm4i",
dap_name=["GaussianModel", "LinearModel"],
dap_parameters={
"GaussianModel": {
"center": {"value": 0.0, "vary": True},
"sigma": {"value": 1.0, "vary": False, "min": 0.0},
}
},
)
Use the same model names as the
LMFit built-in models classes, such as GaussianModel,
LinearModel, or ConstantModel.

